# Deny everything, then allow what the image actually needs.
#
# The other way round -- listing what to exclude -- fails silently and expensively here.
# `data/panel.db` holds every account's password and session in plaintext (ADR-0003), so one
# forgotten line bakes credentials into an image layer, where deleting the file later does
# not remove it. A missed *allow* only breaks the build, which is loud and free.
#
# Two more reasons this stays a deny-list: `.local/` is a ~500MB browser download and
# `.browser_profiles/` holds live logged-in sessions. Neither belongs in a build context.
#
# Anything new the image needs has to be allowed below on purpose.
*

# The panel itself. `panel/tests/` comes along so an image can be checked without a second
# build -- pytest is not installed (it is a dev dependency), so add it for the one command:
#
#   docker run --rm <image> sh -c "pip install --user -q pytest pytest-asyncio \
#     && cd /app && python -m pytest panel/tests -q"     # 188 passed
#
# `panel/tests` explicitly, not bare pytest: pytest.ini also lists `tests/`, the desktop
# shell's own suite, whose target modules are deliberately not in this image.
!panel/
!run.py
!requirements.txt
!requirements-browser.txt

# The SPA is built inside the image (stage 1), from source only -- `frontend/dist/` and
# `node_modules/` stay out so the image never inherits a stale or host-built bundle.
!frontend/package.json
!frontend/package-lock.json
!frontend/index.html
!frontend/vite.config.ts
!frontend/tsconfig.json
!frontend/tailwind.config.js
!frontend/postcss.config.js
!frontend/src/

# No rule for the vendored cloakbrowser helpers: they live in `panel/vendor/`, so `!panel/`
# above already carries them and their BSD-2 notice (clause 2 asks a redistribution of that
# code to reproduce it, and a published image is one).

# Docs the container's own logs point at when something fails.
!CONTEXT.md
!pytest.ini

# Re-exclude what the allows above would otherwise drag back in.
**/__pycache__/
**/*.pyc
**/.pytest_cache/
